WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.282
Hello, and welcome to my project presentation.

2
00:00:03.282 --> 00:00:12.494
My name is Izolde Springe, and this is Dirt and Pine, my final project for the Website Development course at Laurea University of Applied Sciences.

3
00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:21.184
In this course we learned to build websites with HTML and CSS, from the basics in the first workshop to Bootstrap in workshop six.

4
00:00:21.184 --> 00:00:29.635
The task of the final project was to create and publish a website of our own, with at least four pages, that also works well on mobile devices.

5
00:00:29.635 --> 00:00:31.763
I chose mountain biking as my topic.

6
00:00:31.763 --> 00:00:35.901
Many people would like to try it, but they do not know where to start:

7
00:00:35.901 --> 00:00:40.215
which trails suit them, what kind of bike they need, or who to ride with.

8
00:00:40.215 --> 00:00:44.825
Dirt and Pine answers those questions in one place, for riders of every level.

9
00:00:45.833 --> 00:00:49.548
Let me start with the overall architecture.

10
00:00:49.548 --> 00:00:53.867
Dirt and Pine is a static website with five pages:

11
00:00:53.867 --> 00:00:57.668
Home, Trails, Gear, Gallery and Join a Ride.

12
00:00:57.668 --> 00:01:00.778
css, so the design stays consistent.

13
00:01:00.778 --> 00:01:03.802
The images are in their own folder.

14
00:01:03.802 --> 00:01:10.367
The site is published with GitHub Pages, straight from my GitHub repository.

15
00:01:11.367 --> 00:01:13.716
The design is inspired by the forest.

16
00:01:13.716 --> 00:01:16.637
CSS custom properties hold the colour palette:

17
00:01:16.637 --> 00:01:20.065
pine green, moss, warm sand and a burnt orange accent.

18
00:01:20.065 --> 00:01:24.001
Headings use the Oswald font and body text uses Source Sans 3.

19
00:01:24.001 --> 00:01:26.160
Every page has the same structure:

20
00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:36.001
a sticky header with the menu, a large banner with a mountain-shaped edge made with clip-path, the content sections, and a footer with links to every page.

21
00:01:37.001 --> 00:01:39.453
The technologies are HTML5 and CSS3.

22
00:01:39.453 --> 00:01:47.489
I used semantic elements such as header, nav, main, section, article and footer, together with accessibility features:

23
00:01:47.489 --> 00:01:52.053
alt texts, a label for every form field and a skip to content link.

24
00:01:52.053 --> 00:01:56.548
Every interactive feature on the site is built without JavaScript.

25
00:01:56.548 --> 00:02:01.997
On the Join a Ride page I also used Bootstrap 5, for an alert and progress bars.

26
00:02:01.997 --> 00:02:07.718
Bootstrap is loaded into its own cascade layer, so my own styles always win over it.

27
00:02:08.734 --> 00:02:10.548
Responsiveness was important.

28
00:02:10.548 --> 00:02:17.554
The grids use repeat with auto-fit and minmax, so the number of columns adapts to the screen without extra code.

29
00:02:17.554 --> 00:02:25.560
Headings scale smoothly with the clamp function, and media queries at 800 and 520 pixels stack the layout on tablets and phones.

30
00:02:26.567 --> 00:02:30.372
On small screens the menu becomes a hamburger button.

31
00:02:30.372 --> 00:02:33.028
It is a hidden checkbox with a label.

32
00:02:33.028 --> 00:02:38.915
checked selector shows the menu, and the three lines of the icon rotate into an X.

33
00:02:39.915 --> 00:02:43.067
The slideshow uses a single keyframes animation.

34
00:02:43.067 --> 00:02:50.553
Each slide gets a different animation delay, so the four photos fade in one after another, and hovering pauses it.

35
00:02:50.553 --> 00:02:51.604
target selector:

36
00:02:51.604 --> 00:02:57.645
clicking a photo changes the address to that photo's id, which makes its large view visible.

37
00:02:58.649 --> 00:03:01.719
The bike type cards on the Gear page flip in 3D.

38
00:03:01.719 --> 00:03:11.954
The card sets a perspective, the inner element keeps its 3D space with preserve-3d, and on hover or keyboard focus it rotates 180 degrees to show the back side.

39
00:03:12.954 --> 00:03:14.929
Now let me show the website itself.

40
00:03:14.929 --> 00:03:18.146
The home page starts with a large banner and two buttons.

41
00:03:18.146 --> 00:03:26.950
Below it are the reasons to ride, numbered automatically with CSS counters, and cards for each part of the site that lift and zoom when you hover over them.

42
00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:29.207
Further down are some facts and a quote.

43
00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:42.264
The Trails page presents the four riding styles, a table that explains the trail difficulty symbols, and the trail rules, again numbered with CSS counters.

44
00:03:45.764 --> 00:03:48.902
On the Gear page, the cards flip when I hover over them.

45
00:03:48.902 --> 00:03:56.521
The essential kit is a custom checklist, and the frequently asked questions are an accordion made with the details and summary elements.

46
00:04:00.031 --> 00:04:02.817
The Gallery page has the automatic slideshow.

47
00:04:02.817 --> 00:04:06.099
In the photo gallery, the captions slide up on hover.

48
00:04:06.099 --> 00:04:09.010
When I click a photo, it opens in the lightbox.

49
00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:13.035
I can move to the next photo with the arrows, and close it again.

50
00:04:16.535 --> 00:04:19.186
The Join a Ride page has the booking form.

51
00:04:19.186 --> 00:04:20.764
It uses many input types:

52
00:04:20.764 --> 00:04:29.348
text, email and phone fields, a drop-down list, a date picker, skill level radio buttons styled as pills, a range slider and checkboxes.

53
00:04:29.348 --> 00:04:31.809
The browser checks the required fields.

54
00:04:31.809 --> 00:04:38.310
Next to the form is the weekly schedule, where Bootstrap progress bars show how many places are booked.

55
00:04:41.810 --> 00:04:43.973
Finally, here is the site on a phone.

56
00:04:43.973 --> 00:04:53.387
The layout stacks into one column, the images scale to the screen, and the menu collapses into the hamburger button, which opens the menu with a short animation.

57
00:04:56.887 --> 00:05:06.589
To summarize, Dirt and Pine is a five-page responsive website built with HTML and CSS, and every page passes the W3C HTML and CSS validators without errors.

58
00:05:06.589 --> 00:05:08.890
The biggest challenge for me was CSS.

59
00:05:08.890 --> 00:05:18.778
Getting the layout and the small details right took a lot of time, and making Bootstrap work together with my own stylesheet needed the cascade layer solution.

60
00:05:18.778 --> 00:05:26.179
I have learned a lot since the first workshop, and now I can build and publish a complete responsive website on my own.

61
00:05:27.187 --> 00:05:30.044
Thank you for your time, and thank you for watching.

